      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-k/q4-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(i) For x &lt; 0, x2 &gt; 0, so the range of f is (−16, ∞) . [A1] As x &gt; 8, the range of g is (−∞, 8) . (a) [A1] (ii) The composite requires x &gt; 8 [B1] and g(x) &lt; 0. This means 12 −x/2 &lt; 0, or −x/2 &lt; −12, hence x &gt; 24. Also f(g(x)) = (12 −x/2)2 −16 . = (x −24)2/4 −16\" style=\"--madas-width:669.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-k/q4-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"Thus the domain is x &gt; 24 , [A1] and g(x) runs through all negative numbers, giving range (−16, ∞) . [A1] (b) The right-hand input also requires 2x −22 &gt; 8, [B1] already true for x &gt; 24. [A1] The right side is g(2x −22). Work through the expansion: 4(x −24)2 −16 = 12 −1 1 2(2x −22) (x −24)2 −64 = 48 −2(2x −22) x2 −48x + 576 −64 = 48 −4x + 44 x2 −44x + 420 = 0 (x −30)(x −14) = 0 Equivalently, the equation becomes (12 −x/2)2 −16 = 23 −x, x2 −44x + 420 = (x −14)(x −30) = 0. [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-k/q4-solution-4.svg\" alt=\"Only x = 30 [A1] is in the composite’s domain: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:380.0px\"></figure>",
