    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-k/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Factor (1/2 −x)−3 = (1/2)−3(1 −2x)−3. Inside the factor 8 the expansion is 1+(−3)(−2x)+[(−3)(−4)/2!](−2x)2+[(−3)(−4)(−5)/3!](−2x)3+O(x4) = 1+6x+24x2+80x3+O(x4). Using 8(1 −2x)−3 and the binomial series gives f(x) = 8 + 48x + 192x2 + 640x3 + O(x4) . [M1] Take out the constant factor [M1] use the negative-power binomial coefficients [M1] substitute the scaled argument [A1] simplify the coefficients through the cubic term (b) Multiply first by a to get 8a + 48ax + 192ax2 + 640ax3, then by bx to get 8bx + 48bx2 + 192bx3, through the cubic term. Adding, the constant and linear terms are 8a + (48a + 8b)x. The quadratic coefficient of (a + bx)f(x) is 192a + 48b, and the cubic coefficient is 640a + 192b. [B1] Thus\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-k/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"192a + 48b = 42, 640a + 192b = 136. [M1] [A1] The source reduces the two equations first, then eliminates b: 32a + 8b = 7 80a + 24b = 17 −96a −24b = −21 80a + 24b = 17 −16a = −4 a = 1 4 32a + 8b = 7 8 + 8b = 7 b = −1 8 Equivalently, subtract four times the first equation from the second to get −128a = −32. [M1] Then a = 1 4 [A1] and b = −1 8 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
