    "id": "madas/alevel-pure-k/q7",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-k/q7-question-1.svg\" alt=\"y = arcsin x , 1 −≤ x ≤. 1 a) By expressing arccos x in terms of y , show that π arcsin x + arccos x = . ( ) 3 2 b) Hence, or otherwise, solve the equation 3arcsin ( x − 1 ) = 2arccos ( x − 1 ) . ( ) 4\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-k/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) For y ∈[−π/2, π/2], π/2 −y ∈[0, π]. [B1] [A1] With y = arcsin x, x = sin y. Hence arccos x = arccos(sin y) = arccos(cos(π/2 −y)). Since cos(π/2 −y) = sin y = x, the principal inverse cosine is π/2 −y . [A1] (b) Thus arccos x + arcsin x = π/2. In the question let y = x −1, so 3 arcsin y = 2 arccos y = 2(π/2 −arcsin y).\" style=\"--madas-width:524.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-k/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Hence 3 arcsin y = π −2 arcsin y, 5 arcsin y = π, and x −1 = y = sin(π/5). In shorthand, set w = arcsin(x −1). [M1] Then 3w = 2(π/2 −w), giving w = π/5, [A1] which lies on the principal branch: [A1] Hence x = 1 + sin(π/5) ≈1.5878. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 7 is 7 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "y = arcsin x , 1 −≤ x ≤. 1 a) By expressing arccos x in terms of y , show that π arcsin x + arccos x = . ( ) 3 2 b) Hence, or otherwise, solve the equation 3arcsin ( x − 1 ) = 2arccos ( x − 1 ) . ( ) 4"
